#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""plinius-greeter: the account picker in front of Phosh.

greetd shows it (default_session, as the user greeter, in phoc) when the phone has more than one
login account, and whenever no session runs, each account with its picture and username (not
the full name). A tap on an account hands its name to root
(/run/plinius-greeter/request, read by plinius-greeter-login.service); greetd then starts that
account's Phosh, which locks itself at once. The greeter itself asks for no password: PIN or
pattern, the waiting after wrong PINs and the biometrics stay those of Phosh's lock screen.

The rest is what a phone needs before anyone logs in: the time, a dark screen after a while
without a touch, the power key turns it on and off, and held down it opens the power menu (power
off, restart) as Phosh's does. Taps vibrate as in Phosh and Stevia (feedbackd's "button-pressed").

The first start: an account without a password yet (/run/plinius-login/first-start, written by
plinius-greeter-config) gets no tiles but one question, a PIN or a password. That is what the lock
screen, Settings and every other question for the password ask from then on. A PIN is typed on
the greeter's own keypad, a password with the on-screen keyboard (Stevia, started for it). The
greeter hands it to root in its own directory (/run/plinius-greeter/password); root sets it and
logs the account in, or says why not (/run/plinius-login/password-result). This screen has no
clock: it looks as the phone will after it, with the installation's background, accent colour and
plinius-ambient's dust (the defaults of the schemas, installation_look(); the greeter's own settings
live in memory only).

For screenshots without a phone: PLINIUS_GREETER_TEST=1 writes no request, and
PLINIUS_GREETER_USERS="name,..." with PLINIUS_GREETER_LAST=name replaces the
accounts (never use real accounts for pictures), PLINIUS_GREETER_FIRST=name shows the first start.

SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
"""

